.TH AFFIX.TOML 5 "2026-06-06" "affix 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
affix.toml \- affix process policy configuration
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B affix.toml
is a TOML file that maps process image names or executable paths to CPU
placement and process-mode policy.
.PP
On Linux, affix does not create the active configuration file automatically.
The project install recipe installs an example at
.B /usr/local/share/affix/affix.example.toml .
Copy it to
.B /etc/affix/affix.toml
for the system service, or to the XDG user configuration path for a user-mode
service.
The user configuration path is
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/affix/affix.toml ,
falling back to
.B $HOME/.config/affix/affix.toml .
Empty or relative XDG and
.B HOME
values are ignored; if no valid absolute user base exists, affix falls back to
the Linux system path.
.SH TOP-LEVEL SETTINGS
.TP
.BI heuristics " = true|false"
Enable or disable database-backed heuristic classification.  If the field is
omitted, the value defaults to
.BR true .
When the
.B heuristics
feature is not compiled, only built-in database-backed static defaults are
available.
.SH PROCESS RULES
Each process rule is a TOML table.  The table name is the image selector.
.PP
On Linux, a selector that contains at least one
.B /
is matched as a full executable path against
.BR /proc/ pid /exe .
A selector without
.B /
is broad by design: it matches the truncated
.BR /proc/ pid /comm
name or the basename of
.BR /proc/ pid /exe .
.SH RULE FIELDS
.TP
.BI affinity " = " string
Portable or numeric affinity expression.  Terms are separated with
.BR + .
Supported portable terms are:
.RS
.TP
.B P
Performance cores.
.TP
.B E
Efficient cores.
.TP
.B LPE
Low-power efficient cores.
.TP
.B LP-E
Alias for
.BR LPE .
.TP
.B C
Cache-preferred core set.
.TP
.B X3D
Alias for
.BR C .
.RE
.IP
Decimal numeric terms select logical processor indexes directly, for example
.B "2+3" .
.TP
.BI affinity_mask " = " integer|string
Explicit affinity bitmask.  The value may be a TOML integer, a decimal string,
or a hexadecimal string with
.B 0x
or
.B 0X
prefix.  Zero is invalid.
.TP
.BI mode " = " string
Process mode.  Valid values are:
.RS
.TP
.B normal
Do not request efficiency, performance, or realtime mode.  This is the default.  The alias
.B none
is also accepted.
.TP
.B efficiency
Request efficiency mode.  If
.B affinity
and
.B affinity_mask
are omitted, affix assigns the portable affinity expression
.BR E+LPE .
.TP
.B performance
Request performance mode.  If
.B affinity
and
.B affinity_mask
are omitted, affix assigns the portable affinity expression
.BR P+E+C .
.TP
.B realtime
Request realtime mode.  If
.B affinity
and
.B affinity_mask
are omitted, affix assigns the portable affinity expression
.BR P+C .
.RE
.SH TABLE FORMS
The direct table form uses the selector as the table name:
.PP
.nf
[systemd-journald]
affinity = "E+LPE"
mode = "efficiency"
.fi
.PP
Full Linux executable paths must be quoted as TOML table names:
.PP
.nf
["/usr/lib/systemd/systemd-journald"]
affinity = "2+3"
mode = "normal"
.fi
.SH POWER PROFILE TABLES
Affix supports profile-specific rule tables for:
.RS
.TP
.B efficiency
Efficiency or power-saver profile.
.TP
.B balanced
Balanced profile.
.TP
.B performance
Performance profile.
.RE
.PP
Profile-specific tables are partial overlays over the top-level rule for the
same selector.  The active operating-system power profile selects one overlay.
Fields present in the selected overlay override the top-level rule.  Fields
missing from the selected overlay inherit from the top-level rule.  Fields still
missing after that merge mean operating-system default behavior for that field,
except that mode-specific affinity defaults can apply:
.RS
.TP
.B mode = "efficiency"
without
.B affinity
or
.B affinity_mask
supplies the default portable affinity
.BR E+LPE .
.TP
.B mode = "performance"
without
.B affinity
or
.B affinity_mask
supplies the default portable affinity
.BR P+E+C .
.TP
.B mode = "realtime"
without
.B affinity
or
.B affinity_mask
supplies the default portable affinity
.BR P+C .
.RE
.PP
For example:
.PP
.nf
["game"]
mode = "realtime"

["game".performance]
affinity = "P+E"
.fi
.PP
In the
.B performance
profile, the effective rule is
.B mode = "realtime"
plus the
.B affinity
field from the overlay.
In other profiles, the top-level rule still applies and only
.B mode = "realtime"
is requested.
.PP
Profile-specific-only rules are valid:
.PP
.nf
[app.performance]
affinity = "P"
.fi
.PP
This does not create a default
.B app
rule.  If the active profile is not
.B performance
and no default rule exists, affix treats affinity for that rule family as
operating-system default.
.SH EFFECTIVE POLICY
Affix distinguishes an absent matching rule family from an effective rule that
resolves to operating-system default.
.PP
If a process has no matching rule family, affix ignores it.  It does not apply
operating-system default policy to arbitrary unrelated processes.
.PP
If a process has a matching rule family but no effective affinity under the
active profile, affinity is operating-system default for that process family.
Untouched processes are left alone.  Processes previously moved by affix into an
affix-owned cgroup may be moved back to their recorded origin.  Systemd units
whose runtime
.B AllowedCPUs=
was changed by affix may be restored to their recorded original value.
.PP
This is not historical restoration of every pre-existing priority or affinity
state.  Affix applies the policy that is effective now.  If no affinity remains
effective, the intended state is operating-system default or the surrounding
systemd envelope.
.SH LINUX SELECTOR CONSEQUENCES
Linux selectors without
.B /
are intentionally broad.  They match both
.BR /proc/ pid /comm
and the executable basename.  This can match processes in different systemd
units, user managers, or session contexts.  Runtime enforcement is still bounded
by ownership semantics: a broad selector does not allow affix to mutate a broad
session scope blindly.
.PP
When a matching Linux process is contained by a specific service unit, affix may
enforce affinity by changing that service unit's runtime
.BR AllowedCPUs= .
The service unit name does not need to match the executable name.  When a
matching process is under desktop app or background lineage, app scopes and app
slices require a unit-name match with the process identity; otherwise affix may
fall back to an affix-owned cpuset cgroup.
Processes only under broad session or manager scopes are skipped unless they
meet one of those narrower criteria.
.PP
Use full executable path selectors when a broad basename would be ambiguous.
Use systemd configuration directly when the intended policy is really for a
whole slice, such as
.BR background.slice ,
rather than for a particular executable image.
.SH EXAMPLES
Broad Linux rule matching both system and user
.B dbus-daemon
instances:
.PP
.nf
[dbus-daemon]
affinity = "2+3"
mode = "normal"
.fi
.PP
Portable low-power rule for journald:
.PP
.nf
[systemd-journald]
affinity = "E+LPE"
mode = "efficiency"
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/affix/affix.toml
Linux system service configuration.
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/affix/affix.toml
Linux user configuration path.  If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, the fallback is
.B $HOME/.config/affix/affix.toml .
If neither base is valid and absolute, affix falls back to
.B /etc/affix/affix.toml .
.TP
.B /usr/local/share/affix/affix.example.toml
Linux example configuration installed by
.B just install .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR affix (8)
